WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.033
-Fred,
it's always so great to see you,

00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:04.734
and it's always so great to have
you back here in New York City.

00:00:04.734 --> 00:00:05.901
You travel the world.

00:00:05.901 --> 00:00:08.767
I know you love
everywhere you go,

00:00:08.767 --> 00:00:10.334
but you're a New Yorker,
through and through.

00:00:10.334 --> 00:00:12.033
-All the way.
-All the way.

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:13.934
And you know,
this city right now,

00:00:13.934 --> 00:00:16.834
one of the things that's sort of
collecting a bad reputation

00:00:16.834 --> 00:00:19.434
is the subway,
and I know a lot of people

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:21.033
are trying to come up
with solutions

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.667
for what's ailing the subway,

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:26.133
but you actually,
I heard you today --

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:27.901
you say you have an alternative
to the subway.

00:00:27.901 --> 00:00:29.367
-I know where you're
going with this, yeah.

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.634
Yeah.

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:33.534
-[ Laughs ] Go ahead.

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:34.701
[ Laughter ]

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:37.567
-I get asked about it a lot.
-Yeah.

00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:39.200
-The problem is the tracks.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.367
That's what we don't look at,
is the actual tracks.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:45.367
They're expensive.
They're hard to maintain.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:46.901
So what we're
trying to do is,

00:00:46.901 --> 00:00:48.200
instead of having, like,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:49.901
one subway go down
the middle of a tunnel,

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:52.701
why don't we have
a couple side-by-side?

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:54.534
And like Uber, like a taxi,

00:00:54.534 --> 00:00:56.200
you get to tell
the subway driver,

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.634
"Hey, I'm going to this street,
I'm going to that one.

00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:00.234
Can you take me there?"

00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.367
They can double back.

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:02.367
They can go --

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:03.634
It's a little --

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:05.234
You know,
that's from another time.

00:01:05.234 --> 00:01:07.868
That's from, like, the '30s,
when things were very like this.

00:01:07.868 --> 00:01:09.033
This is a different time.

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:10.534
Let's get these trains
out there.

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:13.701
Let's get it all loose,
you know what I'm saying?

00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:15.033
We've got to stop --
-So, wow.

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:18.234
This seems like it's gonna make
it a bigger problem,

00:01:18.234 --> 00:01:22.200
because like, right now,
each train goes one place

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.601
and they're having
a huge problem with this.

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:25.968
And you would say,
your solution is,

00:01:25.968 --> 00:01:29.133
every train could go
wherever the people...

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:31.300
So it's not like, you don't even
look at a map anymore

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.234
to say, like, "Oh, I need the
Orange line or the Green line

00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:34.334
or the Red Line"?

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:36.234
-Yeah, but it's something
to be discussed.

00:01:36.234 --> 00:01:37.767
That's what the problem is.

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:39.501
They're not talking.

00:01:39.501 --> 00:01:44.400
There's no talking going on
with the subway companies.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.267
-See, right away,
I think one of the problems is,

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.167
you're saying things like
"the subway companies."

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:49.400
Like there's...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:50.701
Like there's more than one,

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:52.701
makes me think that maybe
you haven't done enough talking.

00:01:52.701 --> 00:01:54.434
-No, maybe not.

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:56.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:57.234
-You know what?

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:58.834
You know what makes
you different

00:01:58.834 --> 00:02:00.534
than the politicians, Freddy?

00:02:00.534 --> 00:02:02.000
You're willing to look inward
and say,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.334
"You know what?
I maybe got this one wrong."

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:04.801
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:06.400
Maybe.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.200
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.567
-Our guest tonight is --

00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.234
Give it up for Fred
one more time.

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:17.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

